# cron/cloud-crontab — the canonical cloud-user crontab. `install-cron.sh` installs THIS
# file verbatim (`crontab cron/cloud-crontab`), so it is the only thing a bare-metal
# restore reproduces. Edit here and run ./install-cron.sh; do NOT `crontab -e`.
#
# RECONCILED 2026-08-04. This file had drifted badly from the live crontab, and the drift
# was one-directional: every operator fix was made with `crontab -e` and never written
# back. By the time it was noticed the canonical copy still carried the OLD Predictonomy
# schedules (since corrected for Ubuntu cron ignoring CRON_TZ) and, worse, still had the
# YOLO Improvement Agent ENABLED after it was deliberately paused on 2026-08-03. Running
# install-cron.sh would have silently resurrected it.
#
# That is the failure mode to guard against: this file is only a source of truth if it is
# written back to. If you change the live crontab by hand, run
#     crontab -l > cron/cloud-crontab
# and commit, or the next DR restore quietly reverts you.

# Predictonomy Improvement Agent — 3 scoped cycles/day. Times below are LOCAL
# (Europe/London), which is the ONLY timezone this cron understands.
# 2026-07-28 (cycle 0318): widened from `23 */3` (8/day = 56/week) — the weekly quota
# pool was exhausted TWICE at that rate (06-18 -> 06-19, 10 cycles; 07-26, 5 cycles),
# every one dying with "You've hit your weekly limit - resets 2am (Europe/London)".
# 2026-07-28 (cycle 0319): FIXED THE PHASING. Ubuntu's cron (3.0pl1-184ubuntu2, Debian
# fork) does NOT implement CRON_TZ — `man 5 crontab` documents no such variable and it is
# accepted as a plain env var, silently ignored as a directive. The `CRON_TZ=UTC` written
# in 0318 therefore did nothing: `23 4` ran at 04:23 BST = 03:23 UTC, i.e. 37 minutes INTO
# the 03:00 UTC econ refresh (46-58m) — exactly the window 0318 meant to clear.
# Fix: schedule in local time, chosen so BOTH offsets are safe. Local 05:23 = 04:23 UTC in
# BST, 05:23 UTC in GMT — after the ~03:58 UTC refresh completion either way, so DST cannot
# drift it back into the window. Same for 13:23 / 21:23. Still 3/day = 21/week.
23 5,13,21 * * * /home/cloud/IdeaProjects/Predictonomy/ops/agent/run-cycle.sh >> /home/cloud/IdeaProjects/Predictonomy/ops/agent/logs/cron.log 2>&1

# Predictonomy postgres-backup health check (ops/agent/check-backup.sh). LOCAL time.
# 2026-07-28 (cycle 0319): was `CRON_TZ=UTC` + `15 1` intending 01:15 UTC, 15m after the
# k8s `0 1 * * *` (UTC) postgres-backup CronJob. CRON_TZ is ignored here (see above), so it
# actually fired 01:15 BST = 00:15 UTC — 45 MINUTES BEFORE the backup it was checking. The
# script demands a dump dated today (UTC), so it FAILED 45/45 times since 2026-06-14 and
# pushed a high-priority ntfy alert every night while backups were in fact healthy.
# Local 02:15 = 01:15 UTC (BST) / 02:15 UTC (GMT) — after the 01:00 UTC backup either way.
15 2 * * * /home/cloud/IdeaProjects/Predictonomy/ops/agent/check-backup.sh >> /home/cloud/IdeaProjects/Predictonomy/ops/agent/logs/cron.log 2>&1

# Vault auto-unseal — keep the minikube Vault unsealed so backups/secrets never stall (N-0006/N-0009)
# @reboot starts the ~10s loop; */5 watchdog restarts it if it ever dies (flock = single instance).
@reboot /home/cloud/Ideaprojects/STEP0/vault-auto-unseal.sh >> /home/cloud/Ideaprojects/STEP0/logs/vault-auto-unseal.log 2>&1
*/5 * * * * /home/cloud/Ideaprojects/STEP0/vault-auto-unseal.sh >> /home/cloud/Ideaprojects/STEP0/logs/vault-auto-unseal.log 2>&1

